      "id": "nc-evaluator-depth",
      "kind": "non_conclusive",
      "expectedDomainOutcome": "non_conclusive:evaluator_error",
      "generator": {
        "kind": "not-chain",
        "depth": 514,
        "proposition": 0
      },
      "why": "A negation chain deeper than the pinned evaluator's own recursion bound. The pair is generated rather than inlined because 514 nodes of literal JSON would obscure the one fact the case is about. The generator is declared here and the producer emits the digest of what it built, so the bytes are still identified. An evaluator that cannot answer is an evaluator error, not an agreement."
    },
    {
      "id": "mf-forward-reference",
      "kind": "malformed",
      "expectedDomainOutcome": "malformed_input",
      "originalRaw": {
        "schema_version": "tl-syntax.formula/v1",
        "semantic_profile": "mltl.closed-trace/v1",
        "root": 1,
        "nodes": [
          {
            "kind": "true"
          },
          {
            "kind": "not",
            "operand": 99
          }
        ]
      },
      "why": "A node table whose operand identifies no preceding node. The engine is required to report this as a malformed input rather than to panic or to emit output, so observing it is a discharged obligation and the row's outcome word stays `malformed` rather than being flattened to `pass`. That word maps to a passing proof, as it does in tl-parse, but for a narrower reason: tl-parse's corpus is malformed by design six times out of seven, whereas here exactly one case is, and the obligation is that the engine names the state instead of producing an answer. The state stays distinguishable in the producer row, in the count oracle below, and in the bytes Quoin retains."
    }
